installation :heshun_reading_house do
  name "The Heshun Reading House"
  tags :computation, :locality, :trade
  prominence :marginal

  prose <<~PROSE
    Four rooms of the old village library at Heshun, west of Tengchong in #{ref :yunnan, "Yunnan"}. The shelving was cleared in 2069 and the long reading tables stayed. Cases now arrive from settlements that keep a #{ref :bonsai_intelligences, "bounded fungal mind"}, and from workshops trying to live with an #{ref :orphan_machines, "orphan machine"} that has grown peculiar to one road or valley.

    Six human writers turn those accounts into lessons that can travel. A Nu valley diagnosis may be rewritten around a Cumberland pump; a dispute over a terrace becomes a quarrel over a loading bay. The writers know most of these trades only through the letters on their tables, which is why each room keeps a mechanic, a clinician or a farmer on seasonal wages.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :trade, heading: "What The House Trades"
    Settlements may pay with a case their own mind could not solve and an account of what happened afterwards. This is how four rooms in a Yunnan village acquired shelves of mine faults, debt quarrels, broken pumps, difficult births and winters from places none of the writers have seen.

    #{ref :the_brokers, "Pearl River houses"} carry bundles south with ceramics and fungal tissue, then west through their intermediaries. Two hundred and forty bundles left Heshun last season. The cheap copies travel farther than the numbered originals and pass beyond the house's trade records. In 2086 one copied lesson taught the same invented river to minds on two continents, the incident now called #{ref :the_false_headwater, "the False Headwater"}.
  PROSE

  log "2026-08-09 — Housed in a village subscription library of the kind the overseas-remittance towns west of Tengchong built for themselves, funded by families trading out of the country and run by a committee that catalogued and lent rather than published. That decided the long tables, the human writers and a trade built from knowledge sent home by people who had gone away."
end
